+# GDM worker-crash root cause, measurements, and the structural fix (2026-09-14)
+
+Scope: the "Oh no. Error running app." failure on the Gene Structure / Conserved
+Domains & Motifs (GDM) module for the 40-protein / ~8,411-aa rice PR-1 workload,
+and the broader class of shared-cloud stability failures the codebase had been
+chasing with per-module thresholds.
+
+## 1. What the symptom actually means
+
+Every pipeline stage in `pages/2_Gene_Structure_Domain_Motif.py` is wrapped in
+`try/except` that turns a Python exception into `st.error`/`st.warning`. A normal
+Python error therefore **cannot** produce "Oh no. Error running app." That screen
+is Streamlit reporting that the **worker process died** — i.e. the Linux OOM
+killer terminated the app's cgroup (SIGKILL) or a native library segfaulted
+(SIGSEGV). The bug is a *process death*, not a traceback.
+
+## 2. Method: measure, don't assume
+
+The vendored native binaries (MEME 5.5.9 core, miniprot, NCBI Datasets) run in the
+analysis container, so we measured peak RSS / wall time directly with a
+`/proc`-based sampler. Synthetic PR-1/CAP-like FASTA (clearly labelled load-test
+fixtures, never biological results) reproduced the family sizes.
+
+## 3. Findings
+
+### 3.1 MEME is not the memory culprit (contradicts the deployed guards)
+
+| Workload                                   | Peak RSS | Wall time |
+|--------------------------------------------|---------:|----------:|
+| MEME, 40 proteins / 8,411 aa (defaults)    | 10.6 MB  | ~15 s     |
+| MEME, 100 proteins / 21,000 aa (defaults)  | 14.3 MB  | ~62 s     |
+| MEME, 40 proteins, nmotifs=20 maxw=100 ANR | 30.6 MB  | ~54 s     |
+| MEME, 300 proteins / 63,000 aa             | ~15 MB   | > 2 min (wall-time bound) |
+
+The deployed guard capped shared-cloud MEME at **20 proteins / 5,000 aa** and the
+vendored wrapper set `ulimit -v 700000` (≈683 MB) — all defending against a
+process that uses ~11–31 MB. The real 40-protein family was *blocked* by this cap
+even though MEME needs ~11 MB for it. MEME memory is a non-issue; the only real
+constraint for the single-core vendored build is **wall time** on large families.
+
+### 3.2 Python post-processing is cheap
+
+`collapse_domains` (O(hits²)/gene) + `domain_qc` + ZIP packaging for 40–300 genes
+adds only ~7–18 MB; ZIP archives are < 0.1 MB. The largest Python allocation is
+the on-demand `combined()` matplotlib figure (~215–240 MB over baseline), which is
+already bounded (`_family_height`) and rendered one-at-a-time.
+
+### 3.3 The real failure mode is structural
+
+Heavy work runs synchronously in the Streamlit worker with **no enforced per-child
+memory ceiling**. Any child that spikes — MAFFT `--auto` (L-INS-i) on a dense
+family, miniprot indexing a large genome, a future larger family, or a runaway
+native tool — can push the cgroup past its limit, and the kernel OOM-kills the
+*entire* cgroup including the parent Streamlit process. There was no mechanism to
+convert a child blow-up into a clean, catchable error, so the app just died.
+
+Compounding factors: shared-cloud detection was duplicated in four places
+(`gdm_phylogeny`, `gdm_cdd_meme`, `gdm_reference`, the shell wrapper) with no
+single source of truth; guards were size guesses untied to measured resource use;
+there was no stage instrumentation, so the true peak/culprit was never measured
+(hence repeated guessing); and a failure in the un-wrapped tail (motif QC,
+ordering, packaging) discarded already-computed upstream results.
+
+## 4. The fix (structural, not another threshold)
+
+- **`modules/runtime.py`** — single source of truth for shared-cloud detection,
+  worker memory budget and thread policy; a `run_guarded()` subprocess runner that
+  launches every native tool under enforced `RLIMIT_AS`/`RLIMIT_CPU` in its own
+  process group; and a `stage()` / `log_event()` instrumentation layer that writes
+  start/end, elapsed, RSS delta and child exit status to stderr (→ Cloud logs).
+- **Every native subprocess** (MAFFT, FastTree, IQ-TREE, MEME, est2genome,
+  miniprot, datasets) now runs through `run_guarded()`. A child that exceeds its
+  ceiling is killed by the kernel and raised as a catchable `ChildResourceError`;
+  **the parent worker survives** and upstream results are preserved. Proven: a
+  memory-starved vendored-MEME child raises `ChildResourceError` while the test
+  process stays alive (`tests/test_runtime_sandbox.py`). The Go `datasets` binary
+  is exempt from the address-space limit (it reserves large virtual memory).
+- **Evidence-based MEME envelope**: raised to 120 proteins / 30,000 aa (a
+  wall-time guard), matched in the Python layer and the shell wrapper, so the
+  40-protein family now runs on cloud. The kernel ceiling is the real backstop.
+- **Preserve upstream results**: the result object is persisted to `session_state`
+  up-front and mutated in place; the previously-unwrapped tail is guarded, so a
+  late failure never blanks completed stages.
+- **Wrapper hardening**: per-invocation `mktemp` runtime dir (was a fixed
+  `/tmp/bps_meme_xxxx` path that collided across concurrent runs and leaked).
+
+Everything degrades gracefully off POSIX (the developer's Windows box): tools still
+run, just without the kernel ceiling, and a one-time notice is logged.
+
+## 5. Residual risks (monitored, not yet closed)
+
+1. **Native ABI segfault in the numeric stack.** `requirements.txt` pins
+   `streamlit==1.63.0` and `pyarrow<25` but leaves `pandas`/`numpy`/`matplotlib`
+   unpinned, so the Python-3.14 cloud pulls the latest (pandas 3.x / numpy 2.x). A
+   pyarrow<25 built against an older ABI can segfault while Streamlit serialises a
+   dataframe for `st.dataframe` — which would only manifest once CDD/MEME populate
+   the domain/motif tables. This could not be reproduced in the analysis container
+   (PyPI egress is blocked here). The new stage instrumentation will pinpoint it:
+   if the worker dies immediately after `run.analysis_done` during table render,
+   pin the numeric stack (verify in a staging deploy before merging the pin).
+2. **MAFFT L-INS-i peak** was not measured locally (MAFFT is not installable in
+   this container). It is now bounded by `run_guarded`'s memory ceiling, but its
+   true peak on a dense family should be confirmed via the instrumentation on the
+   next real run.
+
+## 6. Recommended stress-test matrix
+
+Run each with CDD + MEME enabled, on the shared-cloud deployment, and read the
+`[bps:...]` lines in the Streamlit Cloud logs (Manage app → logs) for per-stage
+elapsed and RSS delta.
+
+| Proteins | Expectation | What to watch |
+|---------:|-------------|---------------|
+| 10  | Fast baseline; MEME + CDD complete in seconds. | `stage.end` RSS deltas small. |
+| 40  | The historical failure case — must now complete (MEME allowed, ~11 MB). | Confirm no worker death; MEME `child.exit rc=0`. |
+| 100 | MEME ~14 MB / ~1 min; still within envelope. | Wall time on the shared worker; CDD batching. |
+| 300 | **Above the MEME envelope (63k aa) → intentionally skipped with a clear notice**; CDD still runs in batches; MAFFT+FastTree only. | Envelope message shown, no subsampling, upstream preserved. |
+| 800 | Large-family route: FastTree, CDD batched, MEME skipped; figures paginated. | Peak RSS vs worker budget; `child.killed` if any tool trips its ceiling (should be a clean error, not "Oh no"). |
+
+The pass criterion at every size is the same: **either the stage completes, or it
+fails with a visible scientific message and the worker stays alive** — never "Oh
+no. Error running app."
